{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>He shall put on the holy linen tunic, etc.</b> At first glance it appears that besides the atonement there is an additional benefit in the entry of the High Priest to see the face of the Shekhinah once a year, in order to strengthen the faith — just as HaShem appeared to all Israel at Mount Sinai in order to implant in their hearts the utterance of ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״, so year by year HaShem wished to reveal Himself to the priest, their emissary, in order to renew the faith in these two commandments which are the foundation of the faith. And perhaps if Israel transgressed ״לֹא תִרְצָח״ and ״לֹא תִנְאָף״, it is because they correspond to the utterance of ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״, as we explained above in parashat Ki Tissa on the verse ״וַיָּקֻמוּ לְצַחֵק״ (Exodus 32:6); therefore the priest was commanded to don a holy linen tunic, for the tunic — our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Arakhin 16a) — atones for the shedding of blood, and the linen breeches atone for sexual immorality, so that they not be opposed to the two commandments of ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״, and to forge with them a new covenant year after year. And since with idolatry the Holy One, blessed be He, punishes even for the thought, therefore it says ״וּבְאַבְנֵט בַּד יַחְגּוֹר״, for the sash atones for the musings of the heart (ibid. 16a). And since at this very entry the sons of Aaron were punished for gazing at the Shekhinah out of a coarse heart, therefore regarding Aaron it says ״וּבְמִצְנֶפֶת בַּד יִצְנֹף״, for the turban atones for arrogance (ibid. 16a)." "Another matter: why these four garments were specified — corresponding to the matters in which the sons of Aaron stumbled. Now the tunic and the sash follow the approach brought in the Yalkut (Shemini, remez 524) that the sons of Aaron would walk behind Moses and Aaron and say: When will these two old men die, and we will rule over the congregation? Rabbi Aibu said: They said this thing with their mouths. Rabbi Pinchas said: They mused it in their hearts. And the question is difficult: on what do they disagree, and who told them that they actually said this, or that they mused it in their hearts?" "And it appears that the opinion of Rabbi Aibu tends to say: since it says ״כְּתֹנֶת בַּד קֹדֶשׁ יִלְבָּשׁ״, and the tunic atones for the shedding of blood — and where do we find that the sons of Aaron stumbled in the sin of shedding blood? Rather, surely they said this thing with their mouths, and they would whiten the face of Moses and Aaron by saying that they are not worthy to be leaders except on account of being old, and one who whitens the face of his fellow is considered as one who sheds blood. Therefore this tunic comes to atone for the shedding of blood, so that Aaron would not enter the Holy and have upon him the suspicion of this sin, for the sons of Aaron were contaminated with this sin yet beheld Elohim. And since ״כִּי לֹא לְפָנָיו חָנֵף יָבֹא״ (Job 13:16) — no evil will come into His dwelling — therefore Aaron was warned about this atonement upon his entry into the Holy, so that he not enter the Holy contaminated with this sin." "And the opinion of Rabbi Pinchas: since it says ״וּבְאַבְנֵט בַּד יַחְגּוֹר״, which atones for musing — surely they mused this in their hearts, according to the way we explained the words of Rabbi Aibu. And Rabbi Aibu holds that the sash atones for musings of sexual immorality, for the sons of Aaron had no wives, and one who is without a wife is full of musings of transgression. And Rabbi Pinchas holds that the tunic, which atones for shedding of blood, is because they did not engage in procreation and propagation, and therefore it is as though they shed blood, as is concluded in Yevamot (63b): It was taught, Rabbi Eliezer says: whoever does not engage in procreation and propagation is as though he sheds blood, as it says (Genesis 9:6) ״שֹׁפֵךְ דַּם הָאָדָם״, and it is written (ibid. 9:7) ״וְאַתֶּם פְּרוּ וּרְבוּ״. And the reason for this is that the blood of his seedlings is demanded from his hand. And ״מִכְנְסֵי בַד״ — which atones for sexual immorality — is because they entered intoxicated with wine, which habituates one to sexual immorality, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 75a): whoever sets his eyes on his cup, all transgressions seem to him level, as it says (Proverbs 23:31) ״כִּי יִתֵּן בַּכּוֹס עֵינוֹ יִתְהַלֵּךְ בְּמֵישָׁרִים״. ״וּבְמִצְנֶפֶת בַּד״ — for it atones for arrogance, and this encompasses several kinds of sin that our Rabbis of blessed memory attributed to them, all dependent on arrogance: both that they gazed at the Shekhinah out of a coarse heart, and that they rendered rulings in the presence of Moses our master, and several kinds of transgressions which we recalled above in parashat Shemini (Leviticus 10:1). And since the sons of Aaron died because they beheld Elohim while still contaminated with these sins, therefore Aaron was commanded that upon his entry to see the glory of HaShem he must be clean of all the contamination of these sins, and he must don these four white garments that whiten these sins — ״אִם יַאְדִּימוּ כַּתּוֹלָע כַּצֶּמֶר יִהְיוּ״. And furthermore, just as when one comes to see the face of the Shekhinah, His garments are like white linen — as we explained above in parashat Shemini on the verse ״וַיַּרְא אֵת אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — so too one comes to see in white garments."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:15.052068712Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}